## Date, Time & Timers — WASM guest clock + scheduler (CHRONO / MOMENT / PERFORMANCE / TIMER) — CODE COMPLETE (release commit in progress)

A new WASM guest-facing API for wall time, monotonic time, calendar values, and scheduled callbacks, plus the host-side clock/civil logic and a dedicated timer agent pool. Code complete across the engine and all three SDKs; `--chrono` host suite + `testapi.wasm` guest tests pass. Being committed/pushed across five repos as this is written.

### The guest object model (deliberately splits JavaScript `Date`'s "two hats")
`Date` conflates a *value* (a fixed instant) with a *clock* (read "now"). Sneeze splits them into four facades reached from the guest `FABRIC`:
- **CHRONO** (`pFabric.Chrono()`) — the wall-clock facade: current wall-time scalars + fresh `MOMENT` objects.
- **MOMENT** — a guest-resident **value object** (a `#[repr(C, packed)]` struct / packed C/C++ struct) that caches the *full civil breakdown* (both UTC and Local) obtained from a single host call. No host-side lifecycle — it lives entirely in guest memory. Mutable setters (JS `Date` semantics): a setter mutates in place and triggers a host recompute/normalize.
- **PERFORMANCE** (`pFabric.Performance()`) — the monotonic-clock facade: high-resolution 100 ns durations + a wall-clock origin.
- **TIMER** (`pFabric.Timer()`) — the scheduler facade: `Set`/`Interval`/`Clear` with `eTIMER_UNIT` (TICK, MS, HZ) and a `qwParam` cookie.

### ABI (`sneeze_abi.h`, shared by all three SDKs)
- `wType` routing added: **CHRONO=9, PERFORMANCE=10, TIMER=11**.
- `SNEEZE_ABI_MOMENT` stores both scalar epochs — **`tm`** (int64, 1/64 s since 1601-01-01 UTC, engine-native) and **`dt`** (int64, ms since 1970-01-01 UTC, web-native) — plus two nested `SNEEZE_ABI_CIVIL` structs (UTC and Local).
- Civil fields follow **Windows `SYSTEMTIME` conventions**: 1-based months, 0-based weekday, full (4-digit) year.
- **`dwFraction`** (uint32, 100 ns units, 0..9,999,999) is the canonical stored sub-second value; `bTick` (1/64 s) and `wMilli` (ms) are derived views.
- One host→guest export only: **`Notify`**. A timer delivery is a `TIMER_FIRED` packet (`wType=TIMER`, `wMethod=TIMER_FIRED`, body = `twFabricIx`, `twTimerIx`, `qwParam`).

### Naming rules (Dean's, enforced across all SDK languages)
- PascalCase `Object_Action`, never camelCase JS names. `Year()` not `FullYear()`.
- **No function overloading anywhere in the guest API** → bare getters `Year()` + `_Set`-suffixed setters `Year_Set(v)`. UTC family suffixed `_Utc` (`Year_Utc()`, `Hour_Utc_Set()`).
- Getters/setters use **`int32_t`** at the interface (to carry JS-`Date` rollover: `Month_Set(13)`, `Date_Set(0)`, negatives); underlying storage stays native narrow types. (Dean noted 13 fits a `uint8_t` — the width is purely for rollover/negative inputs, accepted.)

### Host implementation — `src/deps/wasm/Chrono.{h,cpp}` (free functions in `namespace SNEEZE::DEP`)
- C++17 `std::chrono` for wall + monotonic clocks. Wall clock: `GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime` (Windows) / `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` (POSIX). Monotonic: `std::chrono::steady_clock` via `SteadyNow100()`.
- Civil breakdown/format/parse from scratch on top of C stdlib: per-OS `gmtime_s`/`localtime_s`, `mktime`/`_mkgmtime`, `strftime`/`sscanf`. **Cross-platform; no IANA tz** (OS local only — accepted). All civil logic is host-side; MOMENT just caches the filled struct.
- **Why the file is named `Chrono` even though it also holds PERFORMANCE code:** mirrors C++ `<chrono>` (which groups wall + monotonic + calendar); the two share epoch/civil helpers. Dean confirmed keeping `Chrono` (rejected `Time.h`/`Clock.h` — `Time.h` risks header collisions). **Naming convention clarified:** `Wasm_*.cpp` files implement `WASM_*` classes declared in the central `Wasm.h`; `Chrono.cpp` and `HostFunctions.cpp` are standalone modules (free functions), so they take no `Wasm_` prefix.

### PERFORMANCE origin is PER-FABRIC (mirrors browser `performance.timeOrigin`, which is per-document)
- Design evolved: global+lazy → (Dean) eager → **per-fabric** after recognizing browsers scope `timeOrigin` per document/iframe. Two fabrics sharing one store each still get their own origin.
- Stored on `FABRIC::Impl`: `m_tmPerfOrigin` (steady) + `m_ft100PerfOrigin` (wall), captured at fabric construction via `DEP::Performance_Origin_Capture(tmSteady, ft100)` (Fabric.cpp includes `wasm/Chrono.h`).
- `include/Scene.h` FABRIC gained `Performance_Origin_Steady()` / `Performance_Origin_Wall()`. **"Wall clock anchor" = `Performance_Origin_Wall()`** = the real-world date/time the monotonic zero corresponds to.
- `Dispatch_Performance` (HostFunctions.cpp) resolves the fabric via `Scene(pWasm_Store)->Fabric_Find(twFabricIx)` and passes its origins into `Performance_Now(tmSteadyOrigin)` / `Performance_Origin(ft100Origin, moment)`. The old global `Performance_Initialize()` in `Wasm_Runtime.cpp` was removed. `tests/ChronoTest.cpp TestPerformance` uses the new per-fabric API (captures its own origin).

### Timer service — `WASM_TIMERS` (engine-wide, owned by `WASM_RUNTIME`)
- Declared in `Wasm.h`; implemented `src/deps/wasm/Wasm_Timers.cpp`. Entry keyed by **(store, twTimerIx)** — the **store**, not one instance, is the timer's home. Members: `std::vector<ENTRY> m_aEntry`, `m_mxTimer`, `m_cvTimer`, `m_twTimer_Next`.
- API: `Arm(store, twFabricIx, eUnit, nValue, qwParam, bRepeat)` → nonzero `twTimerIx` (0 on invalid unit/value); `Clear(store, twTimerIx)`; `Claim(FIRE&)` hands one due/not-in-flight/not-cancelled entry to an agent (earliest-due first), marks it in-flight; `Complete(FIRE&)` reschedules a repeat one period on (**clamped forward** so a slow fire never bursts a backlog: `tpDue += dPeriod; if (tpDue <= now) tpDue = now + dPeriod;`) or erases a one-shot/cleared/closing entry; `Store_Close(store)` cancels the store's entries and blocks until no in-flight fire remains, so the store can be deleted safely.
- `Period(eUnit, nValue)` converts to a `steady_clock::duration`: TICK = n × 1/64 s, MS = n ms, HZ = 1/n s; non-positive → zero (Arm rejects).
- **Firing** = the dedicated multi-agent **TIMER pool**: `AGENT::TIMER` in `src/sneeze/control/AgentTimer.cpp`, woken by the Control metronome (~1000 Hz). `Tick()`: `while (pTimers->Claim(fire)) { fire.pStore->Notify_Timer(...); pTimers->Complete(fire); }`. `WASM_STORE::Notify_Timer` builds the `TIMER_FIRED` packet once and fans it out to every active instance via `WASM_INSTANCE::Notify_Guest` (the Alloc/Memory_Write/Notify/Free handshake mirroring the Open snapshot push).

### Concurrency model (must-understand for future work)
- A wasmtime **`Store` is not thread-safe**, so the per-store `WASM_STORE::m_mutex` serializes **every** host→guest entry into a store: `Instance_Open`, `Instance_Close`, `Instance_Find`, and `Notify_Timer` all take it. Granularity is **per-store, not per-timer** — two timers of one store never run concurrently; different stores run in true parallel across the 4 agents.
- **`WASM_INSTANCE::Notify_Guest` trusts the caller to hold the store lock** (its comment says so). **Forward-looking footgun:** any *future* host→guest push (network file-changed, storage-unit-changed, a future `OnMessage`) MUST take the store `m_mutex` before calling `Notify_Guest`, or it races timers. Today the only caller is `Notify_Timer` (locked).
- `m_mutex` is **non-recursive** → a host function must never synchronously re-enter a locked store method on the same thread (would self-deadlock). This is why guest-triggered fabric spawns are deferred through an async fetch, not called inline. Keep the "post it, don't call it inline" discipline for any new guest-triggered store op.
- `AGENT::TIMER::Tick`'s `Engine()->Wasm_Runtime()` / `->Timers()` null checks are **defensive only, never hit in practice**: the runtime is created (Engine.cpp:78) *before* CONTROL (Engine.cpp:98, which owns the TIMER agents), and CONTROL is deleted (~Impl:168) *before* the runtime (~Impl:184) — so an agent only exists while the runtime exists. If engine `Initialize()` succeeds, both `m_pWasm_Runtime` and `m_pTimers` are non-null for the whole initialized lifetime (a runtime `Initialize()` that returns true has already allocated `m_pTimers`). In the no-app-data-path / WASM-disabled config, CONTROL is never created, so no TIMER agent runs at all.

### KNOWN ISSUE — TIMER-pool head-of-line blocking (PERF ONLY, DEFERRED — Dean: "fix that later")
`Claim` picks the earliest-due entry **regardless of store**, and an agent marks its entry in-flight **before** it calls `Notify_Timer`. So if two due timers belong to the **same** store, two agents can claim them and the second **parks on that store's lock** — committed to its claimed fire, unable to help other stores — until the first delivery finishes. With the 4-agent pool that is **~25%** of timer-drain capacity lost per blocked agent (**up to ~75%** if three pile on one store). It is transient (clears when `Complete` runs), **same-store only** (different stores never contend), and **never a correctness bug or a stall**. **Fix (deferred):** give `Claim` **per-store single-flight** — skip any store that already has an in-flight entry, so a second agent finds other work (or returns to the metronome) instead of blocking. No lock-side change; purely a smarter `Claim`. Recorded in `src/deps/wasm/Wasm.md` ("Known limitation" para) and a `TODO(perf)` block on `WASM_TIMERS::Claim` in `Wasm_Timers.cpp`.

